 Margin Trading Facility, or MTF trading , lets you buy shares by paying only a portion of the value upfront while the broker funds the rest, charging daily interest on the funded amount. Since this interest accrues every day a position stays open, the rate a broker charges directly decides how much of your gain MTF eats into, making it one of the most important numbers to check before you open a demat account and start trading on leverage. 
 Your MTF cost comprises the following charges: 
 Interest rate structure: Most brokers charge either a flat annual rate or a slab-based rate that drops as your funded amount grows. A lower headline rate does not always mean a lower total cost, since some brokers offset it with higher per-order brokerage on MTF trades. 
 Leverage and funding limit: Leverage decides how much of the trade value the broker funds. Among the brokers compared here, that ranges from 4x to 5x depending on the stock and broker (see the table below for each broker's specific figure). Funding limits, the maximum amount you can borrow, vary widely between brokers and often scale with your account profile and collateral. 
 Additional charges: Beyond interest, MTF trades usually carry a separate brokerage charge per order. Shares bought using MTF are subject to applicable pledge requirements, and bokers may charge separate pledge or unpledge fees. These costs can add to the overall expense of an MTF position. 
 The ranking below is built around one primary lens: the annual MTF interest rate, since it is the cost that compounds daily for as long as a funded position stays open and applies regardless of whether the trade itself is profitable. A two or three-percentage-point gap in the annual rate can outweigh differences in brokerage or leverage over any holding period longer than a few weeks. 
 Brokerage per order, leverage, and funding limit are shown alongside the rate for context, not folded into the ranking itself. A broker with a higher funding ceiling or higher leverage isn't necessarily cheaper to use; it's simply able to fund larger or more leveraged positions. Where a broker uses per-day or slab-based pricing instead of a flat annual rate, we've converted it to an approximate annual equivalent so it can be compared on the same basis, and flagged where the effective rate depends on how much you borrow. 
