 Buzzing Stocks: HDFC Bank gains 2% on MD & CEO appointment; D-Mart slips 3% as Citi retains 'sell' rating
 TCS shares added 2% after the company announced an agreement with Best Buy to transition its Global Capability Center (GCC).
 Moneycontrol News 
 October 05, 2026 / 10:08 IST 
 --> 
 Buzzing Stocks 
 Stock-specific activity is likely to remain in focus on Dalal Street on October 5, following a slew of corporate developments, monthly data and brokerages view.
HDFC Bank shares added 2% after RBI approved the appointment of Anup Bagchi as MD & CEO of the bank for a three-year term effective October 27. Bagchi is currently MD & CEO of ICICI Prudential Life Insurance.
Shares of DLF shares gained nearly 2% after the company announced the complete sell-out of The Aureva, its luxury residential project in Gurugram, with sales of around Rs 1,985 crore.
Aurobindo Pharma shares touched 52-week high after the company received final USFDA approval to manufacture and market Perampanel tablets in strengths ranging from 2 mg to 12 mg in the US. The product is bioequivalent and therapeutically equivalent to the reference listed drug Fycompa and will be launched in Q3FY27.
Share price of TCS shares added 2% after the company announced an agreement with Best Buy to transition its Global Capability Center (GCC) in India to TCS, combining the GCC's retail and enterprise expertise with TCS' technology, engineering, AI and global delivery capabilities.
Shares of Avenue Supermarts shed more than 3 percent after Citi maintained 'sell' rating on the stock with a target Rs 3300.
Rail Vikas Nigam shares gained more than 1% despite East Coast Railway cancelled an order for the supply, installation, testing and commissioning of an IP-based Video Surveillance System for rolling stock, without any financial repercussions and with mutual consent.
Punjab National Bank shares gained 3% despite the company's secretary received an administrative warning letter from SEBI's Investigations Department over insider trading regulation violations involving three designated persons and their immediate relatives. SEBI's letter is cautionary and does not impose any financial penalty on the bank.
Investors are expected to remain focused on brokerage calls, block deals, order wins, regulatory developments and other company-specific triggers that could drive stock-level volatility during the session.
