 'There could be a 25bps hike but it’s not the start of the rate hike cycle': Neelkanth Mishra, Executive Director, World Bank 
 India's trend growth remains solid at over 7 percent.
 Global headwinds may slow growth by up to 50 bps.
 Falling rupee does not indicate structural economic issues.
 Did our AI summary help? 
 Market Mastery
As world economies, including India, battle headwinds of high energy prices, an ongoing war, supply chain disruptions, Neelkanth Mishra, Executive Director World Bank tells Moneycontrol’s Shweta Punj that a slowdown is imminent and could shave off upto 30bps of growth.
However, the impact of drought-like conditions would not be as stark since mix of agriculture has changed. On the rupee, Mishra has iterated that a falling rupee is not indicative of a structural issue in the Indian economy. And a rate hike in the US and other economies does not warrant a rate hike in the Indian context. He has also said that India's fertiliser supplies are not as badly disrupted, farmers in richer countries like Australia and America could be more severely impacted. Edited Excerpts.
Punj: Do you see the strong growth momentum from the first quarter to continue or should we brace for a slowdown?
Mishra: We have a very strong trend growth. And after a 10 year down cycle in real estate, we have finally seen a revival over the last 4 or 5 years. And these cycles tend to run a while. So once the housing construction cycle picks up, then it tends to run a while. And that's a very important driver. So I think trend growth is 7% plus for sure, in my view. In the last couple of years, the government was doing a lot of fiscal tightening. Fiscal tightening is a headwind to growth. And the fiscal tightening phase is over. In fact, what we're seeing this year is flat fiscal with no headwind. And next year, potentially, we could see the pay commission implementation happening. This year will be flattish in terms of fiscal deficit, but next two years it's quite possible that the fiscal debt issue itself will be high. Which means that there will be a fiscal boost to growth. Now, this year, with the RBI getting liquidity right. And FCNR flows are helping liquidity as well. The credit growth has finally accelerated. So as credit grows, it helps growth pick up. So some of the underlying domestic growth drivers are very strong..I would expect the momentum to continue. The headwinds from new problems that have emerged. First being the headwind from higher energy prices. Some of that is being offset by higher services growth, but still there is going to be maybe half a percent or one percent worth of a shock which cannot be offset by fiscal interventions. Oil prices, fertiliser and edible oil all that is maybe 2% of GDP - is the headwind. Some of that is being offset by physical intervention. There will be some growth slowdown incrementally whether it is 30 bps that we will have to see.
Punj: How severe could the impact of poor monsoons or super El-nino be on growth?
Mishra: Given the levels of irrigation we have and that we started the year with decent reservoir levels. Sowing of Kharif has been quite okay. I think it’s down only one percent. There will be some yield impact. But as India's agriculture itself has changed. Agriculture is still 70-80%of GDP. The importance of crop agriculture, which is what Kharif crop is all about, is a lot lower than it used to be. So in terms of tonnage now, fruit and vegetables are bigger than grains. Dairy and poultry meat, et cetera. Fisheries. They've also become much larger. So I don't think agricultural output, I mean, it may not grow at 4-5% as it did but it will definitely not be a significant drag on growth.
The El-Nino is just about started. Last week of September, the rains were very heavy. if this continues for another couple of weeks, then maybe you'll have decent soil moisture for Rabi sowing as well. We have to be careful because if reservoir levels are low, and of course, in several parts already, drought has been declared, then Rabi sowing could be a bit of an issue. Rabi is a smaller crop than kharif generally. But I'm not sure that by itself will be a significant growth impact. If we have another monsoon failure next year, then we are talking about serious issues. Then there could be water availability problems for power plants, for various types of industries, for construction. So those will become serious issues.
